  'rafter-length': {"title":"Rafter Length Calculator: Horizontal Run & Rise","metaDesc":"Free Rafter Length Calculator: enter the Horizontal Run and Rise and get a rafter length of 13.00 ft. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/construction/rafter-length","cat":"construction","catName":"Construction","lsi":["rafter length calculator roof pitch run rise framing","rafter length formula","how to calculate rafter length","rafter length formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator works out the rafter length from the Horizontal Run and Rise, following standard Construction conventions — the page defaults produce a rafter length of 13.00 ft.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Horizontal Run and Rise.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the rafter length, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Construction formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (horizontal run of 12, rise of 5), rafter length calculator returns a rafter length of 13.00 ft. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Rafter Length Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator computes the rafter length directly from your inputs — the Horizontal Run and Rise feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator turns the values you enter into a verified output — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Horizontal Run</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Rise</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>The output panel in rafter length calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Explore. Each input change recalculates instantly; watching the rafter length move tells you which factor dominates your case.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator substitutes the Horizontal Run and Rise into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the rafter length rounded for readability.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with horizontal run of 12, rise of 5, this rafter length calculation returns <strong>Rafter Length: 13.00 ft</strong>. The same run reports Pitch: 5.0/12 — order 10-15% extra for overhangs, ridge cuts, and waste.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: Rafter = √(run² + rise²) — the Pythagorean theorem</li><li>12² + 5² = 144 + 25</li><li>√169 = 13.00 ft</li><li>Add the overhang measured along the same slope, not horizontally</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from rafter length calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Rafter Length Calculator: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Rafter Length Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the result. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Rafter Length Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Results from Rafter Length Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because it is fast and private — Rafter Length Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Rafter Length Calculator answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the rafter length? Enter the Horizontal Run and Rise, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath. Because the rafter length arrives with supporting figures and a full step list, the page gives you context rather than a single bare number."},{"q":"How is the result calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: rafter = √(run² + rise²) — the pythagorean theorem, then 12² + 5² = 144 + 25. The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Rafter Length Calculator makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the figure is hidden."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Rafter Length Calculator?","a":"The Horizontal Run and Rise it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the rafter length instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the rafter length calculator returns is the rafter length for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Rafter Length Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Rafter Length Calculator include planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. On this page, rafter length calculator applies the standard Construction method to your inputs and lists every step of the working beside the result."}]},
