  'sleep-calc': {"title":"Sleep Cycle Calculator: 90-Minute Cycle Bedtime","metaDesc":"Free Sleep Cycle Calculator: get the optimal bedtime instantly, with every step of the math shown. Nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/everyday/sleep-calc","cat":"everyday","catName":"Everyday Life","lsi":["sleep cycle calculator wake up time","sleep cycle formula","how to calculate sleep cycle","optimal bedtime formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Sleep Cycle Calculator works out the optimal bedtime from the Wake Time, following standard Everyday Life conventions — the page defaults produce a optimal bedtime of 22:30 (5 cycles) | 00:00 (4 cycles) | 01:30 (3 cycles).</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Wake Time.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the optimal bedtime, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Everyday Life formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (wake time of 06:00), sleep cycle calculator returns a optimal bedtime of 22:30 (5 cycles) | 00:00 (4 cycles) | 01:30 (3 cycles). Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Sleep Cycle Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Sleep Cycle Calculator computes the optimal bedtime directly from your inputs — the Wake Time feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Sleep Cycle Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Sleep Cycle Calculator answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the result? Enter the Wake Time, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Wake Time</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li>The output panel in sleep cycle calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the figure shows which lever matters most for your sleep cycle question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Sleep Cycle Calculator makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the optimal bedtime is hidden.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with wake time of 06:00, this sleep cycle calculation returns <strong>22:30 (5 cycles) | 00:00 (4 cycles) | 01:30 (3 cycles)</strong>. The same run reports Wake: 06:00 | Sleep cycles are ~90 min.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Wake target: 06:00 (360 min after midnight)</li><li>5 full cycles × 90 min = 450 min before wake → bedtime 22:30</li><li>4 full cycles × 90 min = 360 min before wake → bedtime 00:00</li><li>3 full cycles × 90 min = 270 min before wake → bedtime 01:30</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from sleep cycle calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Sleep Cycle Calculator fits planning and checking: day-to-day planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the optimal bedtime, or any moment when the figure needs to be right the first time.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Rounding intermediate values by hand introduces error Sleep Cycle Calculator does not have; it keeps full precision internally, so trust the displayed optimal bedtime over mental arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Sleep Cycle Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the result is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Results from Sleep Cycle Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Sleep Cycle Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Sleep Cycle Calculator is built for sleep cycle questions that need a defensible number: the working is always visible, the inputs accept your own values, and the result updates as you type. Because the working is visible: Sleep Cycle Calculator shows each operation behind the output in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the optimal bedtime calculated?","a":"The first steps are wake target: 06:00 (360 min after midnight), then 5 full cycles × 90 min = 450 min before wake → bedtime 22:30. Sleep Cycle Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the optimal bedtime can be checked line by line."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Sleep Cycle Calculator?","a":"The Wake Time it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the optimal bedtime instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the sleep cycle calculator returns is the optimal bedtime for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Sleep Cycle Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the figure proportionally."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Sleep Cycle Calculator include day-to-day planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the optimal bedtime — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. Run Sleep Cycle Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows."}]},
