  'alcohol-calories': {"title":"Alcohol Calorie Calculator: Volume, ABV & Number of Drinks","metaDesc":"Free Alcohol Calorie Calculator: enter the Volume and ABV and get calories of 98 kcal. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/food/alcohol-calories","cat":"food","catName":"Food & Nutrition","lsi":["alcohol calorie calculator beer wine spirits ethanol","alcohol calorie formula","how to calculate alcohol calorie","calories formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Alcohol Calorie Calculator works out the calories from the Volume, ABV, and Number of Drinks, following standard Food & Nutrition conventions — the page defaults produce calories of 98 kcal.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Volume, ABV, and Number of Drinks.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the calories, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Food & Nutrition formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (volume of 12, abv of 5, number of drinks of 1), alcohol calorie calculator returns calories of 98 kcal. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Alcohol Calorie Calculator computes the calories directly from your inputs — the Volume, ABV, and Number of Drinks feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Alcohol Calorie Calculator works</h2>\n<p>This page is a working alcohol calorie calculator: enter your values, read the output, and follow the step list to see exactly how the answer was derived.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Volume</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>ABV</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Number of Drinks</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>The output panel in alcohol calorie calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the figure shows which lever matters most for your alcohol calorie question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Rounding follows standard display conventions — the underlying math keeps several decimal places until the calories is shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with volume of 12, abv of 5, number of drinks of 1, this alcohol calorie calculation returns <strong>98 kcal</strong>. The same run reports 14 g pure ethanol | 7 kcal/g | real drinks add carbs (beer ≈ +50 kcal/12 oz).</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Volume = 12 oz × 29.57 = 355 mL</li><li>Ethanol = 1 × 355 × 5% = 17.7 mL</li><li>Grams = 17.7 × 0.789 = 14.0 g</li><li>Calories = 14.0 × 7 = 98 kcal</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from alcohol calorie calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Alcohol Calorie Calculator fits planning and checking: short-term planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the calories, or any moment when the result needs to be right the first time.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Alcohol Calorie Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the output. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Bookmark this page — after the first visit it works offline, so the calories is one tap away even without a connection.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Results from Alcohol Calorie Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Alcohol Calorie Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Alcohol Calorie Calculator turns the values you enter into a verified output — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it. Because the working is visible: Alcohol Calorie Calculator shows each operation behind the calories in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the calories calculated?","a":"The first steps are volume = 12 oz × 29.57 = 355 ml, then ethanol = 1 × 355 × 5% = 17.7 ml. Alcohol Calorie Calculator substitutes the Volume, ABV, and Number of Drinks into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the calories rounded for readability."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Alcohol Calorie Calculator?","a":"The Volume, ABV, and Number of Drinks it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the calories instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the alcohol calorie calculator returns is the calories for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Alcohol Calorie Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the figure proportionally."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Alcohol Calorie Calculator include short-term planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the calories — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. Run Alcohol Calorie Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the result is, which a single run never shows."}]},
