  'bayes-theorem': {"title":"Bayes Theorem Calculator: P Prior, P & P","metaDesc":"Free Bayes Theorem Calculator: enter the P Prior and P and get a update probability of P(A|B) = 15.38%. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/math/bayes-theorem","cat":"math","catName":"Math","lsi":["bayes theorem calculator conditional probability","bayes theorem formula","how to calculate bayes theorem","update probability formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Bayes Theorem Calculator works out the update probability from the P Prior, P, and P, following standard Math conventions — the page defaults produce a update probability of P(A|B) = 15.38%.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> P Prior, P, and P.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the update probability, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Math formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (p prior of 0.01, p of 0.9, p of 0.05), bayes theorem calculator returns a update probability of P(A|B) = 15.38%. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Bayes Theorem Calculator computes the update probability directly from your inputs — the P Prior, P, and P feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>This page is a working bayes theorem calculator: enter your values, read the update probability, and follow the step list to see exactly how the answer was derived.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>P Prior</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li>Note the update probability. It updates as you type, and the worked steps below it make the arithmetic auditable.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the output shows which lever matters most for your bayes theorem question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Rounding follows standard display conventions — the underlying math keeps several decimal places until the update probability is shown.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with p prior of 0.01, p of 0.9, p of 0.05, this bayes theorem calculation returns <strong>P(A|B) = 15.38%</strong>. The same run reports P(B) = 5.85%.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>P(B) = P(A)P(B|A) + P(¬A)P(B|¬A) = 0.0585</li><li>P(A|B) = P(A)P(B|A)/P(B) = 0.1538</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from bayes theorem calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Bayes Theorem Calculator fits planning and checking: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the update probability, or any moment when the output needs to be right the first time.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Bayes Theorem Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the update probability. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Bayes Theorem Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the result is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Bayes Theorem Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the result proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because it is fast and private — Bayes Theorem Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Bayes Theorem Calculator turns the values you enter into a verified update probability — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it. Because the page doubles as documentation: Bayes Theorem Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator."},{"q":"How is the update probability calculated?","a":"The first steps are p(b) = p(a)p(b|a) + p(¬a)p(b|¬a) = 0.0585, then p(a|b) = p(a)p(b|a)/p(b) = 0.1538. Bayes Theorem Calculator substitutes the P Prior, P, and P into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the result rounded for readability."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Bayes Theorem Calculator?","a":"The P Prior, P, and P it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the update probability instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the bayes theorem calculator returns is the update probability for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Bayes Theorem Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Bayes Theorem Calculator include planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the update probability — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. Run Bayes Theorem Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows."}]},
