  'law-of-cosines': {"title":"Law of Cosines Calculator: Side a, Side b & Included Angle C","metaDesc":"Free Law of Cosines Calculator: enter the Side a and Side b and get a result of Side c = 6.67. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/math/law-of-cosines","cat":"math","catName":"Math","lsi":["law of cosines calculator side angle","law cosines formula","how to calculate law cosines","third side formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Law of Cosines Calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Law of Cosines Calculator works out the third side from the Side a, Side b, and Included Angle C, following standard Math conventions — the page defaults produce a result of Side c = 6.67.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Side a, Side b, and Included Angle C.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the third side, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Math formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (side a of 8, side b of 11, included angle c of 37), law of cosines calculator returns a result of Side c = 6.67. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Law of Cosines Calculator computes the third side directly from your inputs — the Side a, Side b, and Included Angle C feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Law of Cosines Calculator is built for law cosines questions that need a defensible number: the working is always visible, the inputs accept your own values, and the result updates as you type.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Side a</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Side b</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li><strong>Included Angle C</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li>The output panel in law of cosines calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the output shows which lever matters most for your law of cosines question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Law of Cosines Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the figure can be checked line by line.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with side a of 8, side b of 11, included angle c of 37, this law of cosines calculation returns <strong>Side c = 6.67</strong>. The same run reports c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos C.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos C</li><li>a² + b² = 64 + 121 = 64121</li><li>2ab·cos C = 2×8×11×cos(37°) = 140.56</li><li>c² = 44.44</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The result panel leads with the third side and follows with intermediate values; if the headline surprises you, the steps usually reveal which input is responsible.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Law of Cosines Calculator: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the third side. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Law of Cosines Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the result. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Law of Cosines Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Results from Law of Cosines Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the working is visible: Law of Cosines Calculator shows each operation behind the third side in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Law of Cosines Calculator calculate?","a":"This page is a working law of cosines calculator: enter your values, read the figure, and follow the step list to see exactly how the answer was derived. Because it is fast and private — Law of Cosines Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed."},{"q":"How is the third side calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos c, then a² + b² = 64 + 121 = 64121. The calculation in Law of Cosines Calculator applies the standard Math method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Law of Cosines Calculator?","a":"The Side a, Side b, and Included Angle C it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the third side instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Law of Cosines Calculator mean?","a":"The main number the law of cosines calculator returns is the third side for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Law of Cosines Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the third side proportionally."},{"q":"When is the Law of Cosines Calculator most useful?","a":"Students, planners, and professionals use it for planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the third side, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else. Bookmark this page — after the first visit it works offline, so the third side is one tap away even without a connection."}]},
