  'paternity-leave': {"title":"US Paid Family Leave Planner: Annual Salary, Leave Weeks","metaDesc":"Free US Paid Family Leave Planner: get the paid family/paternity leave instantly, with every step of the math shown. Nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/family/paternity-leave","cat":"family","catName":"Parenting & Family","lsi":["paid family leave calculator paternity us state","us paid family formula","how to calculate us paid","paid family/paternity leave formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>US Paid Family Leave Planner works out the paid family/paternity leave from the Annual Salary, Leave Weeks, and Weekly Benefit Cap, following standard Parenting & Family conventions — the page defaults produce a paid family/paternity leave of $5,538.462 for 6 wks.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Annual Salary, Leave Weeks, and Weekly Benefit Cap.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the paid family/paternity leave, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Parenting & Family formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (annual salary of 80,000, leave weeks of 6, weekly benefit cap of 1,600), us paid family leave planner returns a paid family/paternity leave of $5,538.462 for 6 wks. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>US Paid Family Leave Planner computes the paid family/paternity leave directly from your inputs — the Annual Salary, Leave Weeks, and Weekly Benefit Cap feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Use US Paid Family Leave Planner when the result needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Parenting & Family method and shows the working, not just the answer.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Annual Salary</strong> — in us paid family leave planner, this value feeds the formula directly, and the steps panel shows exactly where it enters the paid family/paternity leave.</li>\n<li><strong>Leave Weeks</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Weekly Benefit Cap</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>The output panel in us paid family leave planner leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the output shows which lever matters most for your us paid family leave planner question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The engine behind US Paid Family Leave Planner evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the figure you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with annual salary of 80,000, leave weeks of 6, weekly benefit cap of 1,600, this us paid family leave planner calculation returns <strong>$5,538.462 for 6 wks</strong>. The same run reports CA: 60% of $1538/wk = $923/wk (cap $1600) → $923/wk.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Weekly wage = $80000 / 52 = $1538.46</li><li>Benefit rate (CA) = 60% → $923.08/wk</li><li>Apply cap → $923.08/wk</li><li>Total for 6 weeks = $5538.46</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>Interpret the paid family/paternity leave against the inputs that produced it — the same number from different inputs can mean different things, which is why the pairing is always shown.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Typical uses for US Paid Family Leave Planner include short-term planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the paid family/paternity leave — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with US Paid Family Leave Planner is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the result. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> If the paid family/paternity leave looks wrong, read the steps panel before re-entering anything; it usually shows exactly where the number departed from expectation.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>US Paid Family Leave Planner assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the output proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the working is visible: US Paid Family Leave Planner shows each operation behind the figure in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Every run of US Paid Family Leave Planner evaluates the Annual Salary, Leave Weeks, and Weekly Benefit Cap you enter, applies the standard Parenting & Family formula, and reports the figure with each step listed for review. Because the page doubles as documentation: US Paid Family Leave Planner puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator."},{"q":"How is the paid family/paternity leave calculated?","a":"The first steps are weekly wage = $80000 / 52 = $1538.46, then benefit rate (ca) = 60% → $923.08/wk. US Paid Family Leave Planner lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the result can be checked line by line."},{"q":"What do I need to use the US Paid Family Leave Planner?","a":"The Annual Salary, Leave Weeks, and Weekly Benefit Cap it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the paid family/paternity leave instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the us paid family leave planner returns is the paid family/paternity leave for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind US Paid Family Leave Planner covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Common scenarios for US Paid Family Leave Planner: short-term planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the paid family/paternity leave. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers. Run US Paid Family Leave Planner twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows."}]},
