  'gratuity': {"title":"Gratuity Calculator: Last Basic + DA, Years of Service","metaDesc":"Free Gratuity Calculator: enter the Last Basic + DA and Years of Service and get a gratuity of ₹3,60,576.92. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/regional/gratuity","cat":"regional","catName":"Regional","lsi":["15 days salary","gratuity formula","how to calculate gratuity","gratuity formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Gratuity Calculator works out the gratuity from the Last Basic + DA, Years of Service, and Extra Months, following standard Regional conventions — the page defaults produce a gratuity of ₹3,60,576.92.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Last Basic + DA, Years of Service, and Extra Months.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the gratuity, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Regional formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (last basic + da of 50,000, years of service of 12, extra months of 6), gratuity calculator returns a gratuity of ₹3,60,576.92. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Gratuity Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Gratuity Calculator computes the gratuity directly from your inputs — the Last Basic + DA, Years of Service, and Extra Months feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Gratuity Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Gratuity Calculator answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the gratuity? Enter the Last Basic + DA, Years of Service, and Extra Months, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath.</p>\n<h2>Using the Gratuity Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Last Basic + DA</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Years of Service</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Extra Months</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li>Note the gratuity. It updates as you type, and the worked steps below it make the arithmetic auditable.</li>\n<li>Adjust and re-run. Change one input at a time to see how sensitive the gratuity is to it — the fastest way to understand what the calculation is doing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Gratuity Calculator makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the result is hidden.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with last basic + da of 50,000, years of service of 12, extra months of 6, this gratuity calculation returns <strong>Gratuity: ₹3,60,576.92</strong>. The same run reports Formula: Last salary × 15 × years / 26 | Eligible ✓ (12.5 yrs) | Tax-free up to ₹20 lakh.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Total service = 12y 6m = 12.5 years</li><li>Gratuity = ₹50,000 × 15 × 12.5 / 26 = ₹3,60,576.92</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The gratuity is the headline answer; the supporting figures beneath it and the step list give the surrounding context needed to judge it.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Gratuity Calculator fits planning and checking: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it, or any moment when the gratuity needs to be right the first time.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Gratuity Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the gratuity. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Gratuity Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Gratuity Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the gratuity proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because it is fast and private — Gratuity Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Gratuity Calculator is built for gratuity questions that need a defensible number: the working is always visible, the inputs accept your own values, and the figure updates as you type. Because the page doubles as documentation: Gratuity Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator."},{"q":"How is the result calculated?","a":"The first steps are total service = 12y 6m = 12.5 years, then gratuity = ₹50,000 × 15 × 12.5 / 26 = ₹3,60,576.92. Gratuity Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the figure can be checked line by line."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Gratuity Calculator?","a":"The Last Basic + DA, Years of Service, and Extra Months it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the gratuity instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the gratuity calculator returns is the gratuity for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Gratuity Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Gratuity Calculator include planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. Run Gratuity Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the result is, which a single run never shows."}]},
