  'half-life': {"title":"Half-Life Calculator: Initial Amount, Half-Life","metaDesc":"Free Half-Life Calculator: enter the Initial Amount and Half-Life and get a remaining of 88.61. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/science/half-life","cat":"science","catName":"Science","lsi":["free half-life calculator","half life formula","how to calculate half life","remaining formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Half-Life Calculator works out the remaining from the Initial Amount, Half-Life, and Time Elapsed, following standard Science conventions — the page defaults produce a remaining of 88.61.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Initial Amount, Half-Life, and Time Elapsed.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the remaining, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Science formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (initial amount of 100, half-life of 5,730, time elapsed of 1,000), half-life calculator returns a remaining of 88.61. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Half-Life Calculator computes the remaining directly from your inputs — the Initial Amount, Half-Life, and Time Elapsed feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Half-Life Calculator works</h2>\n<p>At its core, Half-Life Calculator takes the Initial Amount, Half-Life, and Time Elapsed and evaluates the standard formula step by step, so the figure can be checked rather than trusted on faith.</p>\n<h2>Using the Half-Life Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Initial Amount</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li><strong>Half-Life</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Time Elapsed</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>The output panel in half-life calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the figure shows which lever matters most for your half-life question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Half-Life Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the remaining can be checked line by line.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with initial amount of 100, half-life of 5,730, time elapsed of 1,000, this half-life calculation returns <strong>Remaining: 88.61</strong>. The same run reports Decayed: 11.39.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: N = N₀ × (1/2)^(t/T)</li><li>t/T = 1000 / 5730 = 0.1745</li><li>(1/2)^0.1745 = 0.8861</li><li>N = 100 × 0.8861 = 88.61</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from half-life calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Typical uses for Half-Life Calculator include planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the remaining — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Half-Life Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the remaining. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Half-Life Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Half-Life Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the result proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Half-Life Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Half-Life Calculator keeps the whole calculation in front of you — the Initial Amount, Half-Life, and Time Elapsed, the formula, the intermediate steps, and a worked example you can reproduce line by line. Because the working is visible: Half-Life Calculator shows each operation behind the figure in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the remaining calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: n = n₀ × (1/2)^(t/t), then t/t = 1000 / 5730 = 0.1745. The calculation in Half-Life Calculator applies the standard Science method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Half-Life Calculator?","a":"The Initial Amount, Half-Life, and Time Elapsed it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the remaining instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the half-life calculator returns is the remaining for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Half-Life Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Common scenarios for Half-Life Calculator: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the remaining. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers. On this page, half-life calculator applies the standard Science method to your inputs and lists every step of the working beside the result."}]},
