  'free-fall': {"title":"Free Fall Calculator: Height & Gravity","metaDesc":"Free Free Fall Calculator: enter the Height and Gravity and get a time of 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/science/free-fall","cat":"science","catName":"Science","lsi":["free fall calculator","fall formula","how to calculate fall","time formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Free Fall Calculator works out the time from the Height and Gravity, following standard Science conventions — the page defaults produce a time of 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Height and Gravity.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the time, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Science formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (height of 100, gravity of 9.8), free fall calculator returns a time of 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Free Fall Calculator computes the time directly from your inputs — the Height and Gravity feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>At its core, Free Fall Calculator takes the Height and Gravity and evaluates the standard formula step by step, so the time can be checked rather than trusted on faith.</p>\n<h2>Using the Free Fall Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Height</strong> — in free fall calculator, this value feeds the formula directly, and the steps panel shows exactly where it enters the time.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>Review the output. Beyond the headline time, the intermediate steps are listed — useful for catching a mistyped input.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the time shows which lever matters most for your free fall question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Free Fall Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the time can be checked line by line.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with height of 100, gravity of 9.8, this free fall calculation returns <strong>Time: 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s</strong>. The same run reports h = ½gt².</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: h = ½gt² → t = √(2h/g)</li><li>t = √(2 × 100 / 9.8) = √20.41</li><li>t = 4.52 s</li><li>vf = g × t = 9.8 × 4.52 = 44.27 m/s</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from free fall calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Typical uses for Free Fall Calculator include planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the time — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Free Fall Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the output. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Free Fall Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The model behind Free Fall Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Free Fall Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Free Fall Calculator keeps the whole calculation in front of you — the Height and Gravity, the formula, the intermediate steps, and a worked example you can reproduce line by line. Because the working is visible: Free Fall Calculator shows each operation behind the time in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the time calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: h = ½gt² → t = √(2h/g), then t = √(2 × 100 / 9.8) = √20.41. The calculation in Free Fall Calculator applies the standard Science method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Free Fall Calculator?","a":"The Height and Gravity it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the time instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the free fall calculator returns is the time for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The time is only as complete as the inputs: anything the page does not ask for (fees, variability, local rules) sits outside the calculation."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Common scenarios for Free Fall Calculator: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the time. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers. Run Free Fall Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the result is, which a single run never shows."}]},
