  'lens': {"title":"Lens Calculator: Focal Length & Object Distance","metaDesc":"Free Lens Calculator: get the image distance instantly, with every step of the math shown. Nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/science/lens","cat":"science","catName":"Science","lsi":["free lens calculator","lens formula","how to calculate lens","image distance formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Lens Calculator works out the image distance from the Focal Length and Object Distance, following standard Science conventions — the page defaults produce a image distance of 15.00 cm | Magnification: -0.50.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Focal Length and Object Distance.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the image distance, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Science formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (focal length of 10, object distance of 30), lens calculator returns a image distance of 15.00 cm | Magnification: -0.50. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Lens Calculator computes the image distance directly from your inputs — the Focal Length and Object Distance feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Lens Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Lens Calculator keeps the whole calculation in front of you — the Focal Length and Object Distance, the formula, the intermediate steps, and a worked example you can reproduce line by line.</p>\n<h2>Using the Lens Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Focal Length</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Object Distance</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li>The output panel in lens calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Explore. Each input change recalculates instantly; watching the image distance move tells you which factor dominates your case.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The calculation in Lens Calculator applies the standard Science method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with focal length of 10, object distance of 30, this lens calculation returns <strong>Image distance: 15.00 cm | Magnification: -0.50</strong>. The same run reports 1/f = 1/do + 1/di.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: 1/f = 1/do + 1/di</li><li>1/di = 1/f - 1/do = 0.1000 - 0.0333 = 0.0667</li><li>di = 1 / 0.0667 = 15.00 cm</li><li>M = -di/do = -15.00/30 = -0.50</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>Read the image distance first, then the steps: together they show not just the value but why that value follows from your inputs.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Students, planners, and professionals use it for planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the image distance, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Lens Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the result. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Lens Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The model behind Lens Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the working is visible: Lens Calculator shows each operation behind the image distance in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Use Lens Calculator when the result needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Science method and shows the working, not just the answer. Because the page doubles as documentation: Lens Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator."},{"q":"How is the image distance calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: 1/f = 1/do + 1/di, then 1/di = 1/f - 1/do = 0.1000 - 0.0333 = 0.0667. The engine behind Lens Calculator evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the image distance you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Lens Calculator?","a":"The Focal Length and Object Distance it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the image distance instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the lens calculator returns is the image distance for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The image distance is only as complete as the inputs: anything the page does not ask for (fees, variability, local rules) sits outside the calculation."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Lens Calculator fits planning and checking: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the image distance, or any moment when the output needs to be right the first time. On this page, lens calculator applies the standard Science method to your inputs and lists every step of the working beside the result."}]},
