  'dew-point': {"title":"Dew Point Calculator: Air Temperature & Relative Humidity","metaDesc":"Free Dew Point Calculator: enter the Air Temperature and Relative Humidity and get a dew point of 23.9 °C. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/science/dew-point","cat":"science","catName":"Science","lsi":["dew point calculator humidity temperature magnus formula condensation","dew point formula","how to calculate dew point","dew point formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Dew Point Calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Dew Point Calculator works out the dew point from the Air Temperature and Relative Humidity, following standard Science conventions — the page defaults produce a dew point of 23.9 °C.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Air Temperature and Relative Humidity.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the dew point, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Science formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (air temperature of 30, relative humidity of 70), dew point calculator returns a dew point of 23.9 °C. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Dew Point Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Dew Point Calculator computes the dew point directly from your inputs — the Air Temperature and Relative Humidity feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Dew Point Calculator is built for dew point questions that need a defensible number: the working is always visible, the inputs accept your own values, and the output updates as you type.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Air Temperature</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Relative Humidity</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>The output panel in dew point calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the figure shows which lever matters most for your dew point question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Dew Point Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the dew point can be checked line by line.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with air temperature of 30, relative humidity of 70, this dew point calculation returns <strong>23.9 °C</strong>. The same run reports Magnus formula | Humidex comfort: below 13 °C dry, 16–21 °C comfortable, above 21 °C muggy.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>alpha = ln(RH/100) + (17.27×T)/(237.7+T) = 1.579</li><li>Dew point = (237.7×alpha)/(17.27−alpha)</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from dew point calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Dew Point Calculator: day-to-day planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Rounding intermediate values by hand introduces error Dew Point Calculator does not have; it keeps full precision internally, so trust the displayed dew point over mental arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> If the dew point looks wrong, read the steps panel before re-entering anything; it usually shows exactly where the number departed from expectation.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Dew Point Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the dew point proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Dew Point Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Dew Point Calculator calculate?","a":"This page is a working dew point calculator: enter your values, read the result, and follow the step list to see exactly how the answer was derived. Because the working is visible: Dew Point Calculator shows each operation behind the figure in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the result calculated?","a":"The first steps are alpha = ln(rh/100) + (17.27×t)/(237.7+t) = 1.579, then dew point = (237.7×alpha)/(17.27−alpha). Rounding follows standard display conventions — the underlying math keeps several decimal places until the dew point is shown."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Dew Point Calculator?","a":"The Air Temperature and Relative Humidity it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the dew point instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Dew Point Calculator mean?","a":"The main number the dew point calculator returns is the dew point for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. The model behind Dew Point Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the Dew Point Calculator most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Dew Point Calculator include day-to-day planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. Run Dew Point Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows."}]},
