  'reading-grade': {"title":"Readability Score Calculator: Total Words, Total","metaDesc":"Free Readability Score Calculator: enter the Total Words and Total Sentences and get a flesch score of 75.7. Every step shown, nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/tech/reading-grade","cat":"tech","catName":"Tech & Digital","lsi":["readability score calculator flesch reading ease grade","readability score formula","how to calculate readability score","flesch score formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the page calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Readability Score Calculator works out the flesch score from the Total Words, Total Sentences, and Total Syllables, following standard Tech & Digital conventions — the page defaults produce a flesch score of 75.7.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Total Words, Total Sentences, and Total Syllables.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the flesch score, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Tech & Digital formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (total words of 100, total sentences of 8, total syllables of 140), readability score calculator returns a flesch score of 75.7. Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Readability Score Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Readability Score Calculator computes the flesch score directly from your inputs — the Total Words, Total Sentences, and Total Syllables feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Use Readability Score Calculator when the flesch score needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Tech & Digital method and shows the working, not just the answer.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Total Words</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li><strong>Total Sentences</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Total Syllables</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>Review the output. Beyond the headline flesch score, the intermediate steps are listed — useful for catching a mistyped input.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the figure shows which lever matters most for your readability score question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The engine behind Readability Score Calculator evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the flesch score you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with total words of 100, total sentences of 8, total syllables of 140, this readability score calculation returns <strong>Flesch score: 75.7</strong>. The same run reports Fairly easy | Grade level ≈ 5.8 | 60–70 is plain English.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: 206.835 − 1.015×(W/S) − 84.6×(Syl/W)</li><li>W/S = 100/8 = 12.50</li><li>Syl/W = 140/100 = 1.40</li><li>Score = 75.7 | Grade ≈ 5.8</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from readability score calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Typical uses for Readability Score Calculator include day-to-day planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the flesch score — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Rounding intermediate values by hand introduces error Readability Score Calculator does not have; it keeps full precision internally, so trust the displayed flesch score over mental arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Readability Score Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The flesch score is only as complete as the inputs: anything the page does not ask for (fees, variability, local rules) sits outside the calculation.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Readability Score Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the tool calculate?","a":"Every run of Readability Score Calculator evaluates the Total Words, Total Sentences, and Total Syllables you enter, applies the standard Tech & Digital formula, and reports the result with each step listed for review. Because the working is visible: Readability Score Calculator shows each operation behind the figure in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the flesch score calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: 206.835 − 1.015×(w/s) − 84.6×(syl/w), then w/s = 100/8 = 12.50. The formula operates on the values exactly as entered; keeping the units shown in each label is what makes the flesch score trustworthy."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Readability Score Calculator?","a":"The Total Words, Total Sentences, and Total Syllables it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the flesch score instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the tool mean?","a":"The main number the readability score calculator returns is the flesch score for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Results from Readability Score Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Common scenarios for Readability Score Calculator: day-to-day planning, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the flesch score. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers. On this page, readability score calculator applies the standard Tech & Digital method to your inputs and lists every step of the working beside the result."}]},
