  'data-center-pue': {"title":"Data Center PUE Calculator: Total Facility Power","metaDesc":"Free Data Center PUE Calculator: get the pue instantly, with every step of the math shown. Nothing leaves your browser.","canonicalPath":"/tech/data-center-pue","cat":"tech","catName":"Tech & Digital","lsi":["pue calculator data center power usage effectiveness efficiency","data center pue formula","how to calculate data center","pue formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does the Data Center PUE Calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Data Center PUE Calculator works out the pue from the Total Facility Power and IT Equipment Power, following standard Tech & Digital conventions — the page defaults produce a pue of 1.30 (good).</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Total Facility Power and IT Equipment Power.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the pue, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Tech & Digital formula, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (total facility power of 130, it equipment power of 100), data center pue calculator returns a pue of 1.30 (good). Assumptions and limits are summarized below.</p><h2>How does the Data Center PUE Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Data Center PUE Calculator computes the pue directly from your inputs — the Total Facility Power and IT Equipment Power feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the math runs locally in your browser and the result appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Data Center PUE Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Data Center PUE Calculator is built for data center pue questions that need a defensible number: the working is always visible, the inputs accept your own values, and the pue updates as you type.</p>\n<h2>Using the Data Center PUE Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Total Facility Power</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>IT Equipment Power</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>The output panel in data center pue calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the output shows which lever matters most for your data center pue question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Data Center PUE Calculator lists every intermediate step in the result panel, so the derivation of the figure can be checked line by line.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with total facility power of 130, it equipment power of 100, this data center pue calculation returns <strong>PUE: 1.30 (good)</strong>. The same run reports Overhead: 30% of IT power goes to cooling, UPS losses, and lighting — every 0.1 reduction saves roughly 88 MWh per year.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: PUE = Total facility power ÷ IT equipment power</li><li>130 kW ÷ 100 kW = 1.300</li><li>Overhead = (PUE − 1) × 100 = 30%</li><li>PUE of 1.0 is the theoretical floor — hyperscale operators reach 1.1, older enterprise halls often sit at 2.0</li></ul><p>Substitute your own values and the same steps produce your answer — that is the point of a calculator that shows its working.</p>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The result panel leads with the pue and follows with intermediate values; if the headline surprises you, the steps usually reveal which input is responsible.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Data Center PUE Calculator: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it. The step list makes it equally useful for learning the method and for double-checking someone else's numbers.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The most common error with Data Center PUE Calculator is a unit mismatch — one value entered in different units than its label assumes quietly skews the pue. Check each label before typing.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Run Data Center PUE Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Very large or very small inputs can push the pue beyond what is practically meaningful — sanity-check extreme values before relying on them.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the working is visible: Data Center PUE Calculator shows each operation behind the output in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Data Center PUE Calculator calculate?","a":"This page is a working data center pue calculator: enter your values, read the figure, and follow the step list to see exactly how the answer was derived. Because it is fast and private — Data Center PUE Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed."},{"q":"How is the result calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: pue = total facility power ÷ it equipment power, then 130 kw ÷ 100 kw = 1.300. Rounding follows standard display conventions — the underlying math keeps several decimal places until the pue is shown."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Data Center PUE Calculator?","a":"The Total Facility Power and IT Equipment Power it asks for, or the page defaults if you just want to see the calculation work. Each input maps directly to the formula, and changing any one of them recalculates the pue instantly."},{"q":"What does the result from the Data Center PUE Calculator mean?","a":"The main number the data center pue calculator returns is the pue for your exact inputs, and the supporting figures and step list give it context. Results from Data Center PUE Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply."},{"q":"When is the Data Center PUE Calculator most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Data Center PUE Calculator include planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking a figure before acting on it — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. Run Data Center PUE Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows."}]},
